resources.string:
"[Principle] The Fourier transform and its inverse of a Gaussian function are both Gaussian functions, so filtering based on Gaussian functions has a special link between the spatial domain and the frequency domain. The passband and stopband transition smoothly.
[Eq. 2-45] H(u,v) = e^{ −D²(u,v) / (2σ²) }
[Eq. 2-46] With σ = D0: H(u,v) = e^{ −D²(u,v) / (2D0²) }
 When D(u,v) = D0, H drops to 0.607 of its maximum.
[Parameters] This demo: D0 = 40.
[Effect] No ringing compared with the ideal low-pass filter and less blur; similar filters include the Butterworth low-pass (Eq. 2-43/2-44, smooth transition and also no ringing) and the trapezoidal low-pass (Eq. 2-47, performance in between the two)."
